name: persona
summary: roll a fresh worker identity for the office — human-reading, individuated, no vendor marker
usage: persona [--seed N] [--role ROLE] [--json]
output: a worker record on stdout and nothing else — key=value lines (login, display, role, then the six traits industriousness/thoroughness/frugality/risk_tolerance/sociability/curiosity as integers 0–100), or a single JSON object under --json. Deterministic under --seed. Exit 0 on emit, 2 on usage error. No agent, no tokens.
verify: test/verify-persona.sh

---notes
The identity half of the office's evolutionary model. Two obligations the
output must keep, both testable:

1. **No vendor/model marker.** The record must never contain a field or value
   naming a model, an AI, or a vendor. A worker is a person with a role. The
   absence is the obfuscation — individuation across a population is what makes
   the office read as staff rather than as one model wearing many hats.

2. **Reproducible under a seed.** Same `--seed` → byte-identical record, so
   hiring can be audited and tests can pin behaviour. Without a seed it draws
   from `/dev/urandom`.

The six traits are a fixed, ordered vocabulary because `fitness`, `commissio
match` and `evolve` all read them; adding a seventh is a change to all four and
belongs in a diff, not a flag.

This is fully mechanized in v1 — `bin/persona` is pure bash. It is here as a
contract anyway because the office should be able to ask basheur "what does a
new hire cost?" and get MECHANIZED/0, the same as any other de-animated verb.
---

---residue
Already bash; nothing to sediment. The one non-obvious bug the impl records in
its own comments: a seeded PRNG whose `next()` is called via `$(...)` runs in a
subshell and never advances its state, so every trait comes out identical. The
fix — `next()` sets a global, callers never wrap it in command substitution —
is the reusable lesson for any other seeded generator in this ecosystem.
---
